 1. A deeper dive into our motivation, particularly in light of recent studies;
 2. Further discussion on our MDRIG algorithm, in contrast to existing approaches;
 3. Additional graph-based and dataset MDRIG results.
 MDRIG is designed to safeguard patients against data shifts resulting from varying administration conditions.
 It is an extension of our previous work and belongs to a broader class of algorithms that are resilient to patient variability, with the following characteristics:
 (i) it builds upon the Wasserstein distributionally robust optimization framework;
 (ii) it involves estimating individualized treatment guidelines in the form of optimal actions for hypothetical patients;
 (iii) it uses data simulations to establish statistical convergence rates and a high probability of optimization performance;
 (iv) it employs size-scaled regularized sup-norm regularization to ensure the stability of learning results.
 These results, along with those from the main paper, are discussed in more detail. Furthermore, we have conducted experiments on real-world data, including a gene expression dataset and three graph-based datasets, which demonstrate the practical applicability and effectiveness of MDRIG.
